<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gaul</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Roman Gaul, Gallia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gaul was the region of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> inhabited by Celtic peoples, roughly present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, Luxembourg and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in stages, culminating in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>'s campaigns of 58–50 BCE, it became a group of prosperous Latin-speaking provinces. From the 5th century it was divided among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, whose kingdom gave France its name.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gaul.jpg" alt="Gaul" width="320" height="323" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Feitscherg · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_Gallia_Tribes_Towns-la.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On the last day of 406, according to the traditional date, Alans crossed the Rhine with the Vandals and Suebi, entered <b>Gaul</b> and in 409 Hispania.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alans">Alans · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Kingship could pass within families: Chnodomar’s nephew Serapio had been given a Greek name by his father, who had learned Greek religious mysteries while a hostage in <b>Gaul</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alemanni">Alemanni · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Romans kept a customs post there, Turicum, on the route between <b>Gaul</b> and Raetia, and a late Roman fort stood on the Lindenhof hill.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canton-of-zurich">Canton of Zürich · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…Herstal, Charles Martel — whose victory near Tours and Poitiers in 732 CE checked an Umayyad raid into <b>Gaul</b> — and Pepin the Short transformed the Carolingians from palace mayors into the effective rulers of the Frankish kingdom, before Pepin deposed the last Merovingian king, Childeric III, and…</blockquote><a href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Duchy of Aquitaine emerged from the break-up of Carolingian authority in south-western <b>Gaul</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-aquitaine">Duchy of Aquitaine · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The name, which first appears in Roman sources in connection with raids into <b>Gaul</b> in the 250s, is usually explained as ‘the bold’ or ‘the fierce’; the later meaning ‘free’ derived from the people’s name, not the reverse.</blockquote><a href="/empire/franks">Franks · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
